{
 "study": "docs/research/MEMORY_LOADING.md",
 "increment": "ml-m1c",
 "previous": "ml-l4b",
 "phase": "M1 (memory): the sprite sheets' file bytes kept compressed in memory",
 "change": "OriginalApplicationStartupInputs.Bitmap keeps its DIB bytes in an OriginalBitmapBytes object: images of 64 KiB or more are compressed with a Swift implementation of the LZ4 block format (OriginalLZ4, matches extended eight bytes at a time) and decompressed on every dib read; smaller or incompressible images keep their bytes. Bitmap gets a hand-written == over the same fields. A surface reads its image once, when it first needs its pixels (77 reads in a whole vs run). The startup package's entries are read, verified and decoded on all cores as the catalog's are (L3's mechanism, paths checked before any read, a file of another size read only in its turn), so its compression adds no startup time.",
 "profile": "Attribution probe and malloc_history at body 600: sprite-sheet file bytes 613 MB (catalog) + 65 MB (arenas) were the largest resident holder; packaged 24-bit sheets 655 MB -> 52 MB with the codec (1.5 GB/s compress on one Mac core); the first gate ml-m1 (without the startup concurrency and the faster match extension) measured A12 startup 2.3 -> 3.2 s and load 46.2 -> 47.3 s: the startup package compressed its 22 large bitmaps (27 MB) serially",
 "review": "independent read-only reviewer: no correctness defect in the codec (always decodable, no out-of-bounds access, malformed input rejected), values and == equivalent, no per-frame decompression; its test fixes applied (a mislabeled malformed case, threshold and fallback cases, inequality cases)",
 "notes": "The first gate ml-m1 also failed one new test: its noise came from an LCG whose low byte repeats every 256 values, so it compressed; the generators are splitmix64 now (suites rerun ml-m1b passed on the corrected tree). Earlier evidence for the same sources: logs/ml-m1-gate.out, logs/ml-m1b-tests.out.",
